// `@onPanic` in a program that also reaches kama's OS seam. The region pulls in `<setjmp.h>`, and the emitted
// C used to include it ABOVE `kama_runtime.h` — whose first act is setting `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` before glibc's
// <features.h> latches its feature set. Read first, <setjmp.h> latched the strict `-std=c11` set, and
// `kama_os.h` then lost `lstat`, `getaddrinfo` and `struct addrinfo` in the same translation unit: eleven
// clang errors, all naming kama's header, none the user's code. Linux/glibc only — the san and linux legs
// are what can see it (consumer KB-25).
import { std::fs::exists };

@noheap @onPanic(recover: 1)
fn int32 guarded(int32 v) {
    return cast<int32>(cast<int8>(v));          // 300 does not fit -> recovered as 1
}

fn int32 main() {
    bool here = exists(path: "/definitely/not/a/path/kama");
    return guarded(v: 300) + guarded(v: 5) + (here ? 100 : 36);   // 1 + 5 + 36 = 42
}
